Kubernetes Resource Dashboard: Panel Guide
A plain-language guide to every panel in the Grafana dashboard "Kubernetes Resource Overview (Cluster, Namespace, Node)".
What the dashboard shows
For each cluster, namespace and node, the dashboard answers four questions about CPU and memory:
• Available: the most Kubernetes lets pods use on a node. This is not free space.
• Requested: what pods asked for when they were set up.
• Limit: the maximum pods are allowed to use. Empty means no limit was set.
• Used: what pods or nodes are using right now.
CPU is shown in cores. Memory is shown in KiB, MiB or GiB. A value shown as "Not reported" means no data arrived, not zero.
Color guide for the percent bars
• Green: under 70%, healthy.
• Orange: 70% to 90%, getting full.
• Red: above 90%, almost out of room.
• Blue (namespace tables): shows how much of the requested amount is actually used. Low usage here is not a problem, but it may mean the team asked for too much.
Controls at the top of the page
• Cluster: a dropdown filled from your data. It drives the Namespace tables and the Quota tables.
• Quota type: chooses CPU or memory, requests or limits, for the Quota tables.
• Time range: defaults to the last 15 minutes. It should be longer than your collector's reporting interval, so rows do not disappear between samples.
Top tiles
• Nodes reporting: how many nodes sent data in the time range.
• Namespaces reporting: how many namespaces have pods sending data.
• Records with cluster name: data samples that say which cluster they came from.
• Records missing cluster name: samples with no cluster name. The tile turns orange when it is above 0. It means the Cluster and Namespace tables are incomplete. Node tables are not affected.
How to read this
A short legend box that explains Available, Requested, Limit, Used and the bar colors.
Section 1: Cluster
Cluster CPU and Cluster Memory tables, with one row per cluster.
Column
Meaning
Cluster
Cluster name
Available
Total the cluster's nodes allow pods to use
Requested
Total pods asked for
Requested % of available
How much of the available amount is already promised
Limit
Total of all limits that were set
Used
Current usage
Used % of available
How much of the available amount is actually in use
Only records with a cluster name are counted, so these tables can be incomplete until the labeling problem is fixed.
Section 2: Namespace
Namespace CPU and Namespace Memory tables, with one row per namespace in the cluster chosen at the top. A namespace is a group of apps, such as payments or checkout.
Column
Meaning
Namespace
Namespace name
Requested
Total its pods asked for
Limit
Total of limits that were set
Used
Current usage of its containers
Used % of requested
Share of the requested amount that is really used
There is no Available column, because capacity belongs to nodes, not namespaces. A low "Used % of requested" suggests the namespace asked for more than it uses, so there may be room to reduce requests.
Section 3: Node
Node CPU and Node Memory tables, with one row per node (server). The columns match the Cluster tables. This is the best place to spot a node that is nearly full.
These tables do not need the cluster name, so they still work when labels are missing. Node Used includes system processes, so it can be higher than the total used by workloads.
Node CPU used over time and Node memory used over time are line charts with one line per node over the selected time range. They show spikes and trends that tables cannot.
Section 4: Namespace quotas (collapsed, optional)
Quotas are caps that an administrator sets on a namespace.
• Quota maximum allowed: the cap.
• Quota already used: how much of the cap is taken.
CPU quotas are in millicores (1000 = 1 core). Memory quotas are in bytes. Several quotas on one namespace are listed separately and never added together. These tables stay empty if no quotas exist or the cluster name is missing.
Setup notes (collapsed)
A checklist for the administrator who imports the dashboard.
1. Import with Dashboards, New, Import, and choose the Elasticsearch data source.
2. Use Grafana 11 or later with X-Pack enabled, because the tables rely on Top Metrics.
3. Open Query Inspector on one table. If a column is still called "Top Metrics", adjust the rename setting in Transformations.
4. Make sure every record has labels.k8s_cluster_name.
5. Compare one node with kubectl describe node to confirm CPU and memory units.
Important limits
• Values are the latest reported per node or container in the selected time range.
• This is reported data, not exact scheduler accounting.
• Missing values are shown as "Not reported", never as zero.
• Physical machine capacity is not collected, so "Available" means allocatable, not total hardware.


